.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536002 true] [17592186157686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186155285 13194139536002 false] [17592186157686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378992 13194139536002 true] [17592186157686 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536002 true] [17592186157686 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139536002 true] [17592186157686 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $λ_{1} = 2 + \\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$.*" 13194139536002 false] [17592186157686 1145 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-