_{1}=4\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -16 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{